  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1157 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSprawling high pressure centered over the Mid-Atlantic will keep\nmild and dry conditions locked in through Saturday. Ensemble\nguidance shows temperatures at 850mb around 10-12C, which is \naround the 80th percentile for mid-November. This will yield \nhigh temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s. Morning lows \nwill be closer to normal values given the strong radiational \ncooling from the very dry soil. Daybreak fog, especially around\nlarger bodies of water, will continue to be a daily occurrence.\n\nBy Sunday, a cold front will move through the region as an H5\ntrough moves into the central Plains. Surface low pressure will\ndevelop in the southern Plains and translate northeast through\nthe Ohio and Tennessee River Valleys. Confidence is increasing\nthat must of the area will rain during the day Sunday. However,\nthe rainfall amounts look very light. Latest guidance only \nshows about a tenth of an inch of rain at most for the great \nmajority of the region, which will do little to ameliorate the \nongoing areas of drought. \n\nFollowing the passage of the cold front, much colder conditions\nare forecast for next week, including Thanksgiving. High\ntemperatures will be around 5-10 degrees below normal, in the\nupper upper 40s to lower 50s. We will need to keep an eye out\nfor a small (10-15%) chance of rain on Wednesday as an inverted\ntrough passes south of the region, but any rain that does fall \nwill be a minor nuisance at best.",
